    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if i put my hand on my throat while saying /z/ i can feel the vibration, but i can't feel it with /s/. is that basically just the difference between air noise and a continuous hum? and does that happen with all voiced vs unvoiced sounds or just fricatives?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, that's correct. The sensation you feel when saying /z/ is similar to feeling a continuous hum due to the vibration of your vocal cords. This vibration creates a more noticeable \"presence\" in the sound.\n\nHowever, this phenomenon is not limited to fricatives; it can be observed in other voiced consonant sounds as well, such as /b/, /d/, and /g/ sounds. But it's especially noticeable in fricatives like /z/ because the airflow is more restricted, creating a stronger contrast between voiced and voiceless sounds. For voiceless sounds, like /p/, /t/, and /k/, there is little to no vibration and therefore less sensation.",
      "source": "target",
